# Azure Provisioning Protocol — Field Reference

This document catalogs all fields that `DataSourceAzure` reads from IMDS
(Instance Metadata Service) and `ovf-env.xml`, along with their security and
lifecycle properties.

## Cloud-init Source Mapping

| Current Source (Azure) | Cloud-init Source |
|---|---|
| MediaContent in CCF (OVF) | `ovf-env.xml` — `LinuxProvisioningConfigurationSet` |
| HostingEnvironmentConfiguration in CCF (OVF) | `ovf-env.xml` — `LinuxProvisioningConfigurationSet` |
| CRP InVMArtifactsProfileBlob | IMDS `compute` / `extended` endpoint |
| NMAgent | IMDS `network` endpoint |

## Source Prefixes

The **Source Path(s)** column in the field table uses the following prefixes:

| Prefix | Endpoint / Mechanism | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `IMDS:` | `http://169.254.169.254/metadata/instance?api-version=2021-08-01&extended=true` | Instance Metadata Service. HTTP GET with `Metadata: true` header. Provides compute, network, and extended metadata. Falls back to API version `2019-06-01` without `extended` if the newer version returns 400. |
| `OVF:` | `/dev/sr0` provisioning media, or IMDS `/reprovisiondata` for PPS re-use | OVF environment XML. On initial provisioning, delivered via `/dev/sr0` (CDROM/ISO). On PPS re-use, fetched from IMDS `/reprovisiondata` endpoint. Parsed by `OvfEnvXml.parse_text()`. Contains `ProvisioningSection` and `PlatformSettingsSection` using the `wa:` (`http://schemas.microsoft.com/windowsazure`) namespace. |
| `WS_GOALSTATE:` | `http://168.63.129.16/machine/?comp=goalstate` | GoalState XML fetched from the WireServer (`168.63.129.16`). Contains container and instance identifiers used for health reporting. |

## Field Inventory

> **Encryption note:** We could encrypt all customer data originating from
> the control plane and leave data generated on the node unencrypted.

| Field Name | Description / Notes | Source Path(s) | Can Be Changed After Provisioning |
|---|---|---|---|
| `adminUsername` | Default admin/system user name. | IMDS: `compute.osProfile.adminUsername`<br>OVF: `ProvisioningSection/LinuxProvisioningConfigurationSet/UserName` | Yes — via *Reset password* from portal |
| `adminPassword` | Admin user password (plaintext). | OVF: `ProvisioningSection/LinuxProvisioningConfigurationSet/UserPassword` | Yes — via *Reset password* from portal |
| `containerId` | Container identifier. Used in health reports and ready/failure signals sent back to the fabric. | WS_GOALSTATE: `Container/ContainerId` | No |
| `customData` | User-provided provisioning payload (Base64-decoded on parse). | OVF: `ProvisioningSection/LinuxProvisioningConfigurationSet/CustomData` | No — for single VMs, custom data cannot be updated in the VM model. For VMSS, custom data can be updated with reimage. See [Custom data and Azure VMs](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/virtual-machines/custom-data) and [Reimage a VM in a scale set](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/virtual-machine-scale-sets/virtual-machine-scale-sets-reimage). |
| `disablePasswordAuthentication` | Boolean controlling SSH password auth. Compared against string `"true"`. | IMDS: `compute.osProfile.disablePasswordAuthentication`<br>OVF: `ProvisioningSection/LinuxProvisioningConfigurationSet/DisableSshPasswordAuthentication` | No — see *Passwordless VMs* design |
| `hasCustomData` | Boolean indicating whether the VM was configured with custom data. Used with config key `experimental_fail_on_missing_customdata` to detect and report failures when OVF is missing custom data that IMDS expects to be present. Added in [PR #6779](https://github.com/canonical/cloud-init/pull/6779). | IMDS: `extended.compute.hasCustomData` | No |
| `hostname` | Also known as `compute.osProfile.computerName`. | IMDS: `compute.osProfile.computerName`<br>OVF: `ProvisioningSection/LinuxProvisioningConfigurationSet/HostName` | Yes — via `Rename-Computer` or `hostnamectl`, but does not change IMDS metadata. See [View and modify hostnames](https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/azure/virtual-machines/linux/update-hostname). |
| `location` | Azure region the VM is running in. | IMDS: `compute.location` | No |
| `network.*` | Full network interface configuration (MAC addresses, IPv4/IPv6 addresses, subnet prefixes). No secrets — may need integrity protection. | IMDS: `network.interface[]` | Yes — network interface settings can be changed. See [Create, change, or delete Azure NICs](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-network-interface). |
| `platformFaultDomain` | Fault domain index for the VM. Exposed via `availability_zone` property. | IMDS: `compute.platformFaultDomain` | No |
| `publicKeys.*` | SSH public keys. IMDS provides `keyData` in OpenSSH format. OVF provides `Fingerprint`, `Path`, and `Value` sub-elements per key — `Value` now contains the key material directly, making certificate retrieval unnecessary. | IMDS: `compute.publicKeys[].keyData`<br>OVF: `ProvisioningSection/LinuxProvisioningConfigurationSet/SSH/PublicKeys/PublicKey/{Fingerprint,Path,Value}` | Yes |
| `preprovisionedVm` | Boolean indicating pre-provisioned VM mode. Default: `false`. | OVF: `PlatformSettingsSection/PlatformSettings/PreprovisionedVm` | No |
| `preprovisionedVmType` | Also known as `compute.extended.ppsType`. Values: `RUNNING`, `SAVABLE`, `OS_DISK`, `NONE`. | IMDS: `extended.compute.ppsType`<br>OVF: `PlatformSettingsSection/PlatformSettings/PreprovisionedVMType` | No |
| `provisionGuestProxyAgent` | Boolean enabling Azure Guest Proxy Agent. Default: `false`. | OVF: `PlatformSettingsSection/PlatformSettings/ProvisionGuestProxyAgent` | No |
| `userData` | Data specified at VM creation for use during or after provisioning (Base64-encoded). Used as fallback when no OVF `customData` is provided. | IMDS: `compute.userData` | Yes — see [User data for Azure VMs](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/virtual-machines/user-data). |
| `vmId` | Unique VM identifier. | IMDS: `compute.vmId` | No |

## Per-Boot Configuration (IMDS reads on every boot)

Cloud-init queries IMDS on each boot to refresh instance metadata. These fields
are read regardless of whether the VM has already been provisioned.

| Field Name | Source |
|---|---|
| `adminUsername` | CRP InVMArtifactsProfileBlob |
| `disablePasswordAuthentication` | CRP InVMArtifactsProfileBlob |
| `hostname` | CRP InVMArtifactsProfileBlob |
| `location` | CRP InVMArtifactsProfileBlob |
| `platformFaultDomain` | CRP InVMArtifactsProfileBlob |
| `publicKeys.*` | CRP InVMArtifactsProfileBlob |
| `userData` | CRP InVMArtifactsProfileBlob |
| `vmId` | CRP InVMArtifactsProfileBlob |
| `hasCustomData` | CRP InVMArtifactsProfileBlob |
| `ppsType` | CRP InVMArtifactsProfileBlob |
| `network.*` | NMAgent |

## Proposal: Eliminating ovf-env.xml

The current provisioning flow depends on `ovf-env.xml` delivered via `/dev/sr0`
(CDROM). This mechanism has several drawbacks: it requires mounting removable
media, parsing XML with multiple namespaces, differs from the JSON-based
IMDS already used for most metadata, and produces an intermediate XML file
(`/var/lib/waagent/ovf-env.xml`) that must be persisted on disk — exposing
secrets such as `adminPassword` and `customData` at rest. Additionally, the
host must maintain a copy of the OVF document for as long as the guest may
need it (e.g. across reboots before provisioning completes), tying up host-side
resources for an indeterminate period. Below are two proposed approaches to
replace it with IMDS-native JSON endpoints.

### Option 1: New unified `/provisiondata` endpoint

Introduce a new WireServer endpoint `/provisiondata` that serves all
provisioning fields as JSON for both initial provisioning and PPS scenarios.
The existing `/reprovisiondata` IMDS endpoint remains untouched for backwards
compatibility.

#### Endpoint specification

The endpoint is served by WireServer (`168.63.129.16`), not IMDS. This takes
advantage of the existing WireServer firewall rules that restrict access to
root-only processes, preventing non-root processes from fetching sensitive
provisioning data during early boot. Individual fields can optionally be
fetched via `/provisiondata/<field>`.

```
# Fetch all provisioning data
GET http://168.63.129.16/provisiondata?api-version=<version>

# Fetch a single field
GET http://168.63.129.16/provisiondata/customData?api-version=<version>
GET http://168.63.129.16/provisiondata/adminPassword?api-version=<version>
```

**Required HTTP headers** (same as existing WireServer convention):

| Header | Value | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `x-ms-agent-name` | `WALinuxAgent` | Agent identification (or future cloud-init identifier) |
| `x-ms-version` | `2012-11-30` (or new version TBD) | API contract version |

**API versioning:** A new `api-version` query parameter value is required
(e.g. `2026-01-01`). WireServer must reject requests with older API versions
that do not understand the `/provisiondata` path, returning `400 Bad Request`.

#### Full response schema

Response for full request (`/provisiondata`):

```json
{
  "adminUsername": "azureuser",
  "adminPassword": "<plaintext-password-or-null>",
  "containerId": "a4f7e2b1-3c8d-4e5f-9a6b-7c8d9e0f1a2b",
  "customData": "<base64-encoded-or-null>",
  "disableAdmin": false,
  "disablePasswordAuthentication": true,
  "hasCustomData": true,
  "hostname": "my-vm",
  "location": "eastus",
  "network": {
    "interface": [
      {
        "ipv4": {
          "ipAddress": [
            {
              "privateIpAddress": "10.0.0.22",
              "publicIpAddress": ""
            }
          ],
          "subnet": [
            {
              "address": "10.0.0.0",
              "prefix": "24"
            }
          ]
        },
        "ipv6": {
          "ipAddress": []
        },
        "macAddress": "000D3AFD5E71"
      }
    ]
  },
  "platformFaultDomain": "0",
  "publicKeys": [
    {
      "keyData": "ssh-rsa AAAA...",
      "path": "/home/azureuser/.ssh/authorized_keys"
    }
  ],
  "preprovisionedVm": false,
  "preprovisionedVmType": "None",
  "provisionGuestProxyAgent": false,
  "userData": "<base64-encoded-or-null>",
  "vmId": "d5e6f7a8-1b2c-3d4e-5f6a-7b8c9d0e1f2a"
}
```

| Field | Type | Nullable | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| `adminUsername` | string | Yes | Default admin/system user name. `null` when `disableAdmin` is `true` (no local admin account created). |
| `adminPassword` | string | Yes | Admin user password (plaintext). `null` when no password is configured or when `disableAdmin` is `true`. |
| `containerId` | string | No | Container identifier. Used in health reports and ready/failure signals sent back to the fabric. Replaces GoalState `Container/ContainerId`. |
| `customData` | string | Yes | User-provided provisioning payload (Base64-encoded). `null` when not configured. |
| `disableAdmin` | boolean | No | When `true`, no local admin account is created. `adminUsername` and `adminPassword` will be `null`. Default: `false`. |
| `disablePasswordAuthentication` | boolean | No | Whether SSH password authentication should be disabled. |
| `hasCustomData` | boolean | No | Whether the VM was configured with custom data. Used with `experimental_fail_on_missing_customdata` to detect missing custom data. |
| `hostname` | string | No | VM computer name (maps to OVF `HostName` / IMDS `osProfile.computerName`). |
| `location` | string | No | Azure region the VM is running in (e.g. `"eastus"`). |
| `network` | object | No | Full network interface configuration. Contains `interface[]` array with per-NIC `ipv4` (addresses + subnets), `ipv6` (addresses), and `macAddress`. Same structure as IMDS `network`. |
| `platformFaultDomain` | string | No | Fault domain index for the VM. Exposed via `availability_zone` property. |
| `publicKeys` | array | No | SSH public keys. Each element contains `keyData` (OpenSSH format) and `path` (target file path). Empty array when no keys configured. Same key material as OVF `Value` and IMDS `keyData`. |
| `preprovisionedVm` | boolean | No | Whether the VM is pre-provisioned. |
| `preprovisionedVmType` | string | No | PPS type enum: `"None"`, `"Running"`, `"Savable"`, `"PreprovisionedOSDisk"`. |
| `provisionGuestProxyAgent` | boolean | No | Whether Azure Guest Proxy Agent should be enabled. |
| `userData` | string | Yes | Data specified at VM creation for use during or after provisioning (Base64-encoded). `null` when not configured. |
| `vmId` | string | No | Unique VM identifier. |

Response for single-field requests — the body is the field's JSON value
directly (no wrapper object). `Content-Type` is always `application/json`.

```
GET /provisiondata/customData
→  "PGJhc2U2ND4="

GET /provisiondata/adminPassword
→  "hunter2"

GET /provisiondata/disablePasswordAuthentication
→  true

GET /provisiondata/publicKeys
→  [{"keyData": "ssh-rsa AAAA...", "path": "/home/azureuser/.ssh/authorized_keys"}]

GET /provisiondata/hostname
→  "my-vm"
```

Nullable fields return `null` when not configured (e.g. `GET /provisiondata/adminPassword` → `null`).

#### HTTP status codes

| Code | Condition | Retryable? |
|---|---|---|
| `200 OK` | Provisioning data returned successfully. | N/A |
| `400 Bad Request` | Unrecognized `api-version` or malformed request. | No |
| `404 Not Found` | VM has already reported ready — endpoint is permanently disabled post-provisioning. Also returned for unknown single-field paths (e.g. `/provisiondata/badField`). | **No** — do not retry; fall back to cached data. |
| `429 Too Many Requests` | Rate-limited — client should retry with backoff. | Yes |
| `500 Internal Server Error` | Transient server error. | Yes |
| `503 Service Unavailable` | Provisioning data is not yet staged by the host — WireServer is not ready to serve it. May include a `Retry-After` header. | Yes — retry with backoff (honour `Retry-After` if present). |

#### Security model

| Concern | Mitigation |
|---|---|
| **Network access** | WireServer (`168.63.129.16`) is reachable only from the guest via host-level networking. The existing `iptables`/`nftables` firewall rules restrict WireServer access to `root`-only processes (`-m owner --uid-owner 0`). Non-root processes cannot reach the endpoint. |
| **Provisioning state gating** | WireServer returns `200` only while the VM has **not** yet reported ready. After the ready signal is sent, the endpoint returns `404`. This ensures secrets (`adminPassword`, `customData`) are not accessible post-provisioning. |
| **Secrets in transit** | Data travels over the host-guest virtual network fabric (not a physical network). The same trust boundary applies as the current OVF-over-CDROM and WireServer GoalState mechanisms. |
| **Secrets at rest** | Cloud-init never persists `adminPassword` to disk in cleartext (hashed before storage, redacted in cached OVF). `customData` is written to instance data with restricted permissions (`0600`). The same handling applies regardless of the source endpoint. |
| **Per-field access** | Callers that need only non-sensitive fields can avoid fetching secrets entirely by using the single-field sub-paths. |

#### Provisioning flow — initial provisioning (PPS type = NONE)

This replaces the current OVF-over-CDROM flow and IMDS metadata queries.
All provisioning data, including network configuration, comes from
`/provisiondata`.

```
Boot
 │
 ├─ 1. DS Detect
 │     Check DMI chassis-asset-tag == "7783-7084-3265-9085-8269-3286-77"
 │     (No /dev/sr0 or OVF seed check required)
 │
 ├─ 2. Setup ephemeral DHCP networking
 │     Timeout: 20 minutes
 │     Acquire lease on primary NIC
 │     Extract WireServer IP from DHCP option 245
 │       (default: 168.63.129.16)
 │
 ├─ 3. (Optional) Check Azure Proxy Agent status
 │     /usr/bin/azure-proxy-agent --status --wait 120
 │
 ├─ 4. Fetch provisioning data from WireServer
 │     GET http://168.63.129.16/provisiondata?api-version=<version>
 │     Headers: x-ms-agent-name, x-ms-version
 │     Retry: on 429/500/503, up to 300-second deadline
 │       (honour Retry-After header from 503)
 │     On 404: VM already provisioned — stop retrying,
 │       use cached data
 │
 ├─ 5. Use containerId from /provisiondata for health reporting
 │     (No GoalState fetch needed — containerId is included
 │      in /provisiondata)
 │
 ├─ 6. Report ready to fabric
 │     POST http://168.63.129.16/machine?comp=health
 │     Body: Health XML with State=Ready,
 │       ContainerId from /provisiondata
 │     (No ISO ejection — /dev/sr0 not mounted)
 │
 ├─ 7. Teardown ephemeral networking
 │
 └─ 8. Apply configuration
       Set hostname, create user, configure SSH keys,
       set/hash password, run user-data/custom-data
```

**Key difference from today:** GoalState is no longer needed —
`containerId` is included directly in the `/provisiondata` response.

#### Provisioning flow — PPS RUNNING (hot-swap with vnet switch)

```
Boot
 │
 ├─ 1. DS Detect + ephemeral DHCP networking
 │
 ├─ 2. Fetch /provisiondata from WireServer
 │     → preprovisionedVm=true, preprovisionedVmType="Running"
 │
 ├─ 3. Create reported_ready marker file
 │     Path: {cloud_dir}/data/reported_ready
 │     Content: "{pid}: {timestamp}"
 │
 ├─ 4. Report ready to fabric (source VM context)
 │     POST health report with State=Ready
 │
 ├─ 5. Wait for vnet switch
 │     Create netlink socket
 │     Wait for media disconnect + reconnect on primary NIC
 │       (netlink.wait_for_media_disconnect_connect)
 │
 ├─ 6. Teardown source ephemeral networking
 │
 ├─ 7. Re-acquire DHCP on re-attached NIC (destination context)
 │     Timeout: 5 minutes per attempt, retry until success
 │
 ├─ 8. Fetch /provisiondata from WireServer (destination context)
 │     Contains the new VM's provisioning data
 │     → preprovisionedVm=false (or new PPS state)
 │
 ├─ 9. Fetch IMDS instance metadata (destination context)
 │
 ├─ 10. Report ready to fabric (destination context)
 │
 ├─ 11. Cleanup reported_ready marker file
 │
 └─ 12. Teardown ephemeral networking + apply configuration
```

#### Provisioning flow — PPS SAVABLE (suspend/resume with NIC hot-attach)

```
Boot
 │
 ├─ 1. DS Detect + ephemeral DHCP networking
 │
 ├─ 2. Fetch /provisiondata from WireServer
 │     → preprovisionedVmType="Savable"
 │
 ├─ 3. Create reported_ready marker file
 │
 ├─ 4. Report ready to fabric (expect transient URL errors)
 │
 ├─ 5. Teardown ephemeral networking (ignore errors)
 │
 ├─ 6. Wait for NIC detach event
 │     (netlink.wait_for_nic_detach_event)
 │
 ├─ 7. Wait for new primary NIC hot-attach
 │     (netlink.wait_for_nic_attach_event)
 │
 ├─ 8. Setup ephemeral DHCP on new NIC
 │
 ├─ 9. Fetch /provisiondata from WireServer (destination context)
 │
 ├─ 10. Fetch IMDS instance metadata (destination context)
 │
 ├─ 11. Report ready to fabric (destination context)
 │
 ├─ 12. Cleanup marker + teardown + apply configuration
```

#### Provisioning flow — PPS OS_DISK (shutdown and redeploy)

```
Boot
 │
 ├─ 1. DS Detect + ephemeral DHCP networking
 │
 ├─ 2. Fetch /provisiondata from WireServer
 │     → preprovisionedVmType="PreprovisionedOSDisk"
 │
 ├─ 3. Report ready to fabric
 │
 ├─ 4. Wait for platform shutdown (sleep indefinitely)
 │     Cloud-init sleeps for 31536000 seconds (1 year)
 │     Platform shuts down VM externally
 │
 └─ On next boot: normal initial provisioning flow
     (marker file absent, PPS type = NONE)
```

#### Provisioning flow — PPS UNKNOWN (recovery reprovisioning)

```
Boot (after previous reported_ready marker exists)
 │
 ├─ 1. DS Detect + ephemeral DHCP networking
 │
 ├─ 2. Detect marker file exists → PPS type = UNKNOWN
 │
 ├─ 3. Report ready to fabric (if not already reported)
 │
 ├─ 4. Fetch /provisiondata from WireServer
 │     (If 404, VM already provisioned — use cached data)
 │
 ├─ 5. Fetch IMDS metadata
 │
 ├─ 6. Cleanup marker + teardown + apply configuration
```

#### Error handling

Cloud-init reports provisioning failures through two channels. The same
reporting mechanism is used regardless of whether data came from
`/provisiondata` or the legacy OVF flow.

| Channel | Mechanism | Details |
|---|---|---|
| **KVP (host)** | `report_via_kvp()` → `/var/opt/hyperv/.kvp_pool_*` | Always attempted. Encoded as `result=error\|success`, `reason=<error-reason>`, `agent=Cloud-Init/<version>`, `vm_id=<vm-id>`, `timestamp=<UTC-ISO>`, `documentation_url=https://aka.ms/linuxprovisioningerror`. |
| **WireServer (fabric)** | POST health report with `State=NotReady`, `SubStatus=ProvisioningFailed` | Attempted if networking is available. If networking is down, cloud-init tears down current networking, attempts new DHCP (20-minute timeout), then reports. |

**Retry strategy for `/provisiondata`:**

| Parameter | Value |
|---|---|
| Retry HTTP codes | `429`, `500`, `503` |
| Retry deadline | 300 seconds from first request |
| Backoff | Exponential, starting at 1 second. Honour `Retry-After` header from `503` responses when present. |
| Max connection errors | 11 (if no route configured for WireServer), unlimited otherwise |
| Non-retryable codes | `400` (bad API version), `404` (already provisioned — **do not retry**) |

**Error classes relevant to `/provisiondata`:**

- `ReportableErrorImdsUrlError` — adapted or new class for WireServer URL
  errors (HTTP code, duration).
- `ReportableErrorOvfParsingException` — replaced by a new
  `ReportableErrorProvisionDataParsingException` for JSON parse failures.
- `ReportableErrorOvfInvalidMetadata` — replaced by a new
  `ReportableErrorProvisionDataInvalidMetadata` for missing or invalid fields.

#### Backward compatibility and migration

| Concern | Approach |
|---|---|
| **Detecting endpoint availability** | Cloud-init attempts `GET /provisiondata?api-version=<version>` first. If WireServer returns `400` (unrecognized API version) or connection refused, fall back to the legacy OVF-over-CDROM flow. |
| **Legacy VMs** | Existing VMs that have already provisioned will never call `/provisiondata`. The endpoint returns `404` post-provisioning regardless. |
| **Mixed fleets** | Cloud-init ships both code paths. The `/provisiondata` path is attempted first; on failure, the `/dev/sr0` OVF path is used. A `datasource.Azure.provisioning_source` config key (`provisiondata` \| `ovf` \| `auto`) can override detection. Default: `auto`. |
| **`/reprovisiondata` coexistence** | The existing IMDS `/reprovisiondata` endpoint continues to work for PPS scenarios during the migration period. Once all PPS types are migrated to use `/provisiondata` in the destination context, `/reprovisiondata` can be deprecated. |
| **GoalState** | No longer required — `containerId` is now included in `/provisiondata`. During migration, cloud-init falls back to GoalState if `/provisiondata` is unavailable. |

#### What `/provisiondata` replaces and what it does not

| Current mechanism | Replaced by `/provisiondata`? | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `/dev/sr0` OVF-env.xml mount + XML parsing | **Yes** | All fields from `ProvisioningSection` and `PlatformSettingsSection` are served as JSON. |
| IMDS `/metadata/instance` | **Yes** | All fields including `network.*`, `vmId`, `location`, `platformFaultDomain`, `userData`, `osProfile`, and `extended` flags are included in `/provisiondata`. IMDS is no longer queried during provisioning. |
| WireServer GoalState (`/machine/?comp=goalstate`) | **Yes** | `containerId` is included in `/provisiondata`. GoalState fetch is no longer needed. |
| GoalState certificate retrieval | **N/A** | OVF already provides key material via `Value` elements — certificate retrieval is no longer used. `/provisiondata` continues to provide keys directly via `keyData`. |
| WireServer health reports (`/machine?comp=health`) | **No** | Health reporting is unchanged. |
| IMDS `/reprovisiondata` | **Eventually** | Coexists during migration. PPS flows can use `/provisiondata` in the destination context instead. |

#### Advantages

- Single JSON request replaces OVF XML parsing entirely — eliminates the
  `OvfEnvXml` parser, XML namespace handling (`wa:`, `dmtf:`), and the
  `defusedxml` dependency for this code path.
- Same endpoint works for both initial provisioning and PPS re-use across all
  PPS types (RUNNING, SAVABLE, OS_DISK).
- No `/dev/sr0` mount required — eliminates ISO device discovery, UDF/ISO9660
  filesystem mounting, CDROM ejection, and the host-side requirement to
  maintain a copy of the OVF document for the lifetime of provisioning.
- SSH public keys are provided as `keyData` directly, consistent with OVF
  `Value` elements and IMDS `keyData` — no format conversion needed.
- Endpoint is gated on provisioning state (returns `404` after ready signal),
  so secrets are not accessible post-provisioning.
- Per-field sub-paths (`/provisiondata/adminPassword`,
  `/provisiondata/customData`) allow fetching only the secrets needed,
  reducing exposure surface.
- JSON schema is self-documenting and strongly typed — no ambiguity around
  boolean-as-string (`"true"` vs `true`) that exists in the current OVF XML.
- Leverages existing WireServer firewall rules (`root`-only `iptables` rules)
  — no new network access control infrastructure required.

#### Disadvantages

- Secrets (`adminPassword`, `customData`) are returned when requesting the full
  `/provisiondata` response, even if the caller doesn't need them — use
  per-field sub-paths to mitigate.
- Requires WireServer to serve provisioning data before the VM reports ready,
  which may have ordering implications with how WireServer currently stages
  GoalState data.
- The endpoint must be gated on provisioning state — WireServer returns the
  data only while the VM has not yet reported ready, and returns `404`
  afterwards. This adds state-tracking responsibility to WireServer.
- Requires maintaining two code paths (JSON `/provisiondata` + legacy OVF XML)
  during the migration period until all Azure regions and VM types support the
  new endpoint.
- WireServer must assemble all provisioning fields into a single JSON
  response, even though they originate from different host-side sources
  (CRP, NMAgent, etc.). This may require coordination on the host side.

### Option 2: Split sensitive fields into on-demand WireServer endpoints

Keep non-sensitive provisioning fields in the existing IMDS instance metadata
(most are already there), and add two new WireServer endpoints for the secrets
that are fetched only when needed:

```
GET http://168.63.129.16/provisiondata/customData?api-version=<version>
GET http://168.63.129.16/provisiondata/adminPassword?api-version=<version>
```

The client uses existing IMDS extended metadata flags to decide whether to
fetch each endpoint:

| Flag | IMDS Path | Controls |
|---|---|---|
| `hasCustomData` | `extended.compute.hasCustomData` | Fetch `/provisiondata/customData` only when `true` |
| `hasAdminPassword` | `extended.compute.hasAdminPassword` (**new**) | Fetch `/provisiondata/adminPassword` only when `true` |

Flow:

1. Query IMDS `/metadata/instance?extended=true` (already done today).
2. If `extended.compute.hasCustomData == true`, fetch WireServer `/provisiondata/customData`.
3. If `extended.compute.hasAdminPassword == true`, fetch WireServer `/provisiondata/adminPassword`.
4. All other provisioning fields (`adminUsername`, `hostname`,
   `disablePasswordAuthentication`, `publicKeys`, `preprovisionedVm`,
   `preprovisionedVmType`, `provisionGuestProxyAgent`) are already available
   in IMDS instance metadata or OVF `PlatformSettingsSection` and would need
   to be surfaced through IMDS as well.

**Advantages:**

- Secrets are fetched only when present, reducing exposure surface.
- Non-sensitive fields are already served by IMDS — minimal new work.
- `hasAdminPassword` follows the same pattern as the existing `hasCustomData`.
- Like Option 1, the `/provisiondata/customData` and
  `/provisiondata/adminPassword` endpoints are disabled after the VM reports
  ready, so secrets are not accessible post-provisioning.

**Disadvantages:**

- Requires a new IMDS flag (`hasAdminPassword`) and two new WireServer
  endpoints.
- The secret endpoints must be gated on provisioning state (same state-tracking
  requirement as Option 1).
- `PlatformSettingsSection` fields (`preprovisionedVm`,
  `preprovisionedVmType`, `provisionGuestProxyAgent`) would also need IMDS
  equivalents if `/dev/sr0` is fully eliminated.
